  "verbalization": "The visible evidence strongly suggests a mechanistic link between flurbiprofen and Phosphodiesterase 6C (PDE6C), as the drug is implicated in the target pathway through multiple distinct molecular entities. Flurbiprofen is directly linked to DMS-79, which is a known target of several compounds, including 4,2',4'-Trihydroxy-3'-(2-Hydroxy-3-Methylbut-3-Enyl) Chalcone and (1R,2R,6R,9S,10R,11R,14S,15S,17R,18S,20S,23R,24S)-6,10,23-trimethyl-4-azahexacyclo[12.11.0.02,11.04,9.015,24.018,23]pentacosane-17,20-diol. These compounds are all capable of targeting PDE6C, indicating a potential mechanism of action involving the inhibition of PDE6C activity. The consistent presence of these targets across different molecular structures supports the idea that flurbiprofen may exert its effects through a similar mechanism, possibly by interacting with PDE6C or modulating its activity. The evidence is robust and aligns with the expected biological role of PDE6C in cellular signaling, making the relationship between flurbiprofen and PDE6C plausible.",
